WAL-logged major-version upgrades
================================

pg_upgrade prepares the new cluster while both versions are available. It
records storage operations and file captures as text commands in
pg_upgrade_relink.tmp. After the prepared data is synced, the frontend
publishes that file as pg_upgrade_relink and leaves the new cluster stopped.
Initialization and catalog-restore WAL are discarded before this publication.

The startup process consumes the commands on the first normal primary start,
before accepting connections or launching background database writers. It
emits START, RELINK and RAWFILE records together with ordinary relation WAL,
then emits COMPLETE and takes the completion shutdown checkpoint. Upgrade
records have transaction ID zero; there is no upgrade transaction or COMMIT.

RELINK describes explicit INHERIT and RECREATE operations. Logged relations
can inherit their old files with link, clone or swap transfer. Copy and
copy-file-range transfers rebuild them with ordinary SMGR create records and
full-page images. New, rebuilt and unlogged relations are recreated in every
mode. Logged relations also capture their free-space and visibility-map forks,
including when their main forks are inherited, so later WAL can reference those
pages. Per-database batches end before another record type is emitted. File
existence, command input, and physical-layout checks remain necessary.

Before startup can emit WAL or change migrated slots, it durably sets the
reserved backupEndPoint value PG_INT64_MAX. The existing control-file layout
is unchanged. This marker rejects an interrupted emission even if the input
file is subsequently lost. Startup clears it only after the completion
checkpoint and durable removal of pg_upgrade_relink. A partially emitted
target cannot be resumed; a retry needs a fresh target and intact old storage.
The usual source-reuse restrictions for link and swap transfers still apply.

The initial redo position retains all startup and upgrade WAL until completion.
Migrated logical slots receive a new decoding snapshot and positions within
that WAL before their state is saved. Emission rejects unexpected files or
input commands rather than treating an incomplete target as a normal cluster.
